---

<Layout title={`Luxury Motor Yacht ${vesselName} Charter | Luna Diving Phuket`}>
  <main class="pcom-wrapper">
    
    <section class="pcom-hero">
      <span class="pcom-subtitle">Classic 90ft Motor Yacht</span>
      <h1 class="pcom-title">{vesselName}</h1>
      <div class="pcom-divider"></div>
      <p class="pcom-tagline">{tagline}</p>
    </section>

    <section class="pcom-specs-container">
      <div class="pcom-specs-grid">
        {technicalSpecs.map((spec) => (
          <div class="spec-item">
            <span class="spec-label">{spec.label}</span>
            <span class="spec-value">{spec.value}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pcom-gallery-section">
      <div class="gallery-slider-outer">
        <div id="slider" class="gallery-slider-inner no-scrollbar">
          {slideshow.map((slide, index) => (
            <div class="gallery-slide">
              <img 
                src={slide.img} 
                alt={slide.text} 
                width="1200"
                height="675"
                loading="lazy"
                decoding="async"
              />
              <div class="gallery-caption">
                <span class="caption-count">Vessel Tour {index + 1} / {slideshow.length}</span>
                <p class="caption-text">{slide.text}</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <button id="prevBtn" class="nav-button prev" aria-label="Previous">
          <svg width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M15.75 19.5L8.25 12l7.5-7.5"></path></svg>
        </button>
        <button id="nextBtn" class="nav-button next" aria-label="Next">
          <svg width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M8.25 4.5l7.5 7.5-7.5 7.5"></path></svg>
        </button>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pcom-content-grid">
      <div class="content-main">
        <h2 class="content-heading">The architect who drew Amanpuri drew one yacht. Then he kept her.</h2>
        <div class="heading-line"></div>
        <p class="paragraph">
          <strong>Ed Tuttle</strong> designed the iconic Aman resorts. He also designed <strong>Maha Bhetra</strong>, and he owned her until his death in 2020.
        </p>
        <p class="paragraph">
          Ninety feet of Thai boatbuilding: a timber hull, three double cabins below, and an open-plan teak saloon that runs the full width of the top deck. Eight knots is her cruising speed, and that is the point of her. She was drawn for long, unhurried passages into the Andaman rather than fast hops between the same four beaches.
        </p>
        <p class="paragraph">
          Thirty years in one family has left its mark on her. The Jim Thompson silks were specified by Tuttle himself, the eighteenth-century Southeast Asian antiques in the saloon are the originals, and the crew has been aboard for decades. She is not a reproduction of a classic yacht. She is one.
        </p>

        <h3 class="sub-heading">A wooden hull, built to wander</h3>
        <p class="paragraph">
          Maha Bhetra was built in Thailand to a design that borrows from the country's own working boats: a long, narrow timber hull with a fine entry, a deep forefoot and a shallow draft of two and a half metres. She carries her accommodation low and her living space high, which is the reverse of most modern charter yachts and the reason she feels like a house rather than a boat.
        </p>

        <h3 class="sub-heading">The Architect: Ed Tuttle (1945 – 2020)</h3>
        <p class="paragraph">
          Edward Burnham Tuttle Jr. was an American architect and interior designer whose work reset the standard for luxury hospitality. Educated at the University of Washington and shaped by Wright and Mies, he founded Design Realization in Paris in 1977 and spent four decades building the visual language of the Aman group: Amanpuri in Phuket, Amanjena in Morocco, and a long list of hotels that made restraint feel like luxury.
        </p>
        <p class="paragraph">
          Maha Bhetra is the only yacht he designed. She carries the same thinking as the resorts: local craft and local materials handled with a very light touch, spaces that open to the weather, and almost no ornament that is not structural. The louvred cabinetry, the proportions of the saloon, and the way the deckhead steps down over the dining table are all his. He commissioned an exclusive Jim Thompson fabric for her cushions that remains aboard today.
        </p>

        <h3 class="sub-heading">The Top Deck & Saloon</h3>
        <p class="paragraph">
          The top deck is one open room. Teak underfoot, teak overhead, and louvred panels that slide back so the saloon and the aft deck become a single seamless space. A round dining table sits at the centre, while the wrapping banquette at the after end takes all six guests at once. The bow carries a teak daybed wide enough for four, under shade that lasts all day.
        </p>

        <h3 class="sub-heading">Three Cabins, All the Same</h3>
        <p class="paragraph">
          There is no master and no compromise cabin. Below are three identical double cabins of twenty square metres each, every one with a separate shower and WC, a vanity, and wardrobes built into a dressing area. Nobody gets the small room. Day charter carries twelve guests; overnight charter sleeps six.
        </p>

        <h3 class="sub-heading">Dining & Life Aboard</h3>
        <p class="paragraph">
          The galley works from the market that morning. Authentic Thai food is what the crew cooks best and what most guests ask for twice, but the kitchen is equally happy with a continental breakfast or a seafood barbecue on the beach.
        </p>

        <h3 class="sub-heading">Water Toys & Exploration</h3>
        <p class="paragraph">
          Because Maha Bhetra draws two and a half metres, she anchors close in. The tender runs the last hundred metres to beaches that day boats never reach, and the kayaks go further into mangroves and hongs.
        </p>
        <ul class="toy-list">
          {waterToys.map(toy => <li>{toy}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="content-sidebar">
        <div class="booking-sticky-box">
          <h3 class="sidebar-heading">Ready to Charter?</h3>
          <p class="sidebar-text">Inquire about availability for the classic 90ft motor yacht Maha Bhetra departing from Phuket Yacht Haven Marina.</p>
          <a href="https://wa.me/66918233120?text=Hi%20Luna%20Diving!%20I'm%20interested%20in%20booking%20Maha%20Bhetra." target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="pcom-btn-primary">
            Inquire via WhatsApp
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Day Charter Pricing Section -->
    <section class="pcom-pricing-section">
      <div class="pricing-header">
        <h2 class="content-heading">Day Charter Rates</h2>
        <p class="paragraph" style="margin-bottom: 8px;">One day, up to twelve guests (Per Charter, Thai Baht & US Dollar)</p>
        <div class="heading-line"></div>
      </div>
      
      <div class="pcom-cabins-list">
        {dayCharterRates.map((rate) => (
          <div class="cabin-rate-card">
            <div class="cabin-main-info">
              <h3 class="cabin-name">{rate.name}</h3>
              <span class="cabin-location">Itineraries: Kai & Naka, Phang Nga Bay, Koh Maiton, Koh Hong</span>
              <p class="cabin-details">{rate.details}</p>
              <span class="cabin-trip-tag">Day Trip</span>
            </div>
            <div class="cabin-price-zone" style="text-align: right;">
              <div style="margin-bottom: 8px;">
